{
  "headline": "Climate Change Authority chair questions economic assessment of Hunter Valley Operations mine extension",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Matt Kean, chair of the Climate Change Authority, has questioned why the New South Wales Independent Planning Commission did not weigh the economic cost of climate change caused by burning coal from the Hunter Valley Operations mine before approving the project to run until 2045. Speaking in an interview on 2GB radio on Thursday, Kean stated that while the commission acknowledged the project would contribute to climate change, it had not estimated the climate-related economic damage in NSW when weighing local costs and benefits. Kean noted evidence suggesting climate-related disasters could cost Australians at least $73bn a year by 2060. The mine's owners, Yancoal and Glencore, forecast that the project could lead to the release of 809m tonnes of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere once the coal is exported and burned, a figure nearly double Australia’s annual climate pollution. The Independent Planning Commission previously determined that the greenhouse gases from the coal mined near Singleton would affect the people, economy, and environment of the Hunter region, NSW, and the world, but concluded those impacts were outweighed by local benefits such as employment, economic activity, royalties, and taxation revenue. NSW Labor premier Chris Minns applauded the decision, describing it as the right decision that is hugely important for the state economy and responsible for thousands of direct and indirect jobs while producing billions of dollars in royalties. Minns did not mention the development's projected climate impact during the interview."
    }
  ]
}

